 ### Edge Cases and Pitfalls
-- **Overfitting:** Fine-tuning on a small number of views can lead to overfitting, resulting in artifacts in novel views [S3].
-- **Initialization Quality:** Poor initialization (e.g., from a noisy point cloud) can lead to slow convergence or local minima [S1, S3].
-- **Memory Usage:** Enabling growing and pruning requires careful management of memory, especially for large scenes [S3].
+*   **Redundancy:** Initial feed-forward Gaussians may be redundant, requiring effective pruning during fine-tuning to reduce memory consumption [S3].
+*   **Overfitting:** Fine-tuning on limited views may lead to overfitting; regularization or multi-view supervision is recommended [S1, S3].
+*   **Style Consistency:** When fine-tuning for style transfer, naive 2D stylization can break multi-view consistency; methods like Ada-GS require training from scratch for each style, limiting usability [S5].
+*   **Volume Modes:** In volume visualization, different visual modes (e.g., density ranges) require separate transfer functions and fine-tuning stages [S4, unofficial].
 
 ## Subtopics
 
 ### Image-space Gaussian Fine-tuning
-A fine-tuning process for volumetric data that accounts for different visual modes by generating multiple radiance fields using different transfer functions [S4, unofficial]. It optimizes a separate Gaussian set $G_h$ for each mode $h$ against rendered images [S4, unofficial]. It sets itself apart from standard fine-tuning by handling multi-modal volumes (e.g., medical scans with varying dynamic ranges) through transfer functions $TF_h$ that isolate specific value intervals of the physical field $F$ [S4, unofficial]. Typically, 5 transfer functions are used per volume [S4, unofficial].
+A fine-tuning process for volumetric data that accounts for different visual modes by generating multiple radiance fields using different transfer functions. It optimizes the Gaussian set against rendered images for each mode. It sets itself apart by handling diverse modalities (e.g., medical scans, simulations) with varying dynamic ranges through mode-specific supervision [S4, unofficial].
+*   **Steps:**
+    1.  Select a series of transfer functions $\{TF_h\}$, where each $TF_h$ is non-zero only within specific value intervals of the physical field $F$ [S4, unofficial].
+    2.  Generate radiance fields $RF_h = TF_h(F)$ for each mode $h$ [S4, unofficial].
+    3.  Render 64 images through volumetric integration for each radiance field to provide supervision data [S4, unofficial].
+    4.  Compute image-space loss and backpropagate to update the Gaussian set $G_h$ [S4, unofficial].
+    5.  After fine-tuning, Gaussians $G_h$ for each mode can be concatenated or separately rendered [S4, unofficial].
+*   **Parameters:** 5 transfer functions are selected for each volume in practice [S4, unofficial].
+*   **Link:** [Image-space Gaussian Fine-tuning](9138-fine-tuning.md#image-space-gaussian-fine-tuning) (internal reference)
 
 ### Ada-GS
-A baseline method that trains vanilla 3D Gaussian Splatting directly on 2D stylized images [S5]. It preserves better details than GS-Ada but requires training from scratch for every new style image, limiting practical usability [S5]. It sets itself apart from style transfer methods that operate on rendered images post-hoc by integrating style supervision directly into the 3DGS optimization loop [S5].
+A baseline method that trains vanilla 3D Gaussian Splatting directly on 2D stylized images. It preserves better details than GS-Ada but requires training from scratch for every new style image, limiting practical usability compared to methods like GSS [S5].
+*   **Steps:**
+    1.  Train vanilla 3DGS using 2D stylized views as ground truth supervision [S5].
+*   **Comparison:** Less consistent than GSS; requires full retraining for new styles [S5].
+*   **Link:** [Ada-GS](9138-fine-tuning.md#ada-gs) (internal reference)
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-- **Time Complexity:** Not given in the sources. [G] Fine-tuning from a generative prior can accelerate convergence compared to training from scratch [S3].
-- **Memory Complexity:** Not given in the sources. [G] Pruning helps manage memory usage [S3].
-- **Performance:** Can achieve photorealism on par with or surpassing other methods when leveraging pretrained weights [S3].
-- **Strengths:** High fidelity, flexible initialization, ability to adapt to specific constraints [S1, S3].
-- **Limitations:** Requires per-scene optimization, which can be computationally expensive for large datasets [S1, S3].
-- **When to use:** When high-fidelity reconstruction is needed for a specific scene, or when adapting a generative prior to a specific object [S1, S3].
-- **When not to use:** When real-time inference is required without per-scene optimization, or when dealing with very large-scale scenes where per-scene optimization is prohibitive [S3].
+*   **Time Complexity:** Depends on the number of Gaussians $N$ and iterations $T$. Fine-tuning typically converges faster than training from scratch due to pretrained priors [S3].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(N)$ for storing Gaussian attributes. Fine-tuning can reduce $N$ via pruning, lowering memory usage [S3].
+*   **Performance:** Achieves photorealism on par with or surpassing other methods when fine-tuning is applied [S3].
+*   **Strengths:** Accelerates convergence, improves geometric consistency, handles sparse/noisy initialization [S1, S3].
+*   **Limitations:** Requires supervision data (images/poses); style transfer variants may lack multi-view consistency or require retraining [S5].
+*   **When to Use:** When high-fidelity reconstruction is needed from limited or noisy data, or when adapting a pretrained model to a specific scene/style [S1, S3, S5].
+*   **When Not to Use:** When real-time training is required without supervision, or when multi-view consistency is critical and style transfer is needed (prefer GSS over Ada-GS) [S5].
 
 ## Background
-Fine-tuning in 3DGS is inspired by techniques in NeRF and CNNs [S1, S2]. In NeRF, per-scene optimization is used for high-quality reconstruction of specific scenes [S1]. In CNNs, fine-tuning involves fixing lower convolutional layers (conv1 to conv3) and fine-tuning remaining convolutional layers and all fully connected layers except the last one, which is trained from scratch [S2]. The application of fine-tuning to 3DGS allows for the refinement of generative priors or sparse initializations to achieve high-quality novel view synthesis [S1, S3].
+Fine-tuning in 3DGS is inspired by techniques in NeRF and CNNs. In NeRF, methods like LOLNeRF and Code-NeRF use per-scene optimization to refine implicit representations [S1]. In CNNs, fine-tuning involves fixing lower layers and training upper layers on specific datasets [S2]. The application to 3DGS leverages these principles to refine explicit Gaussian representations, often starting from feed-forward predictions or point clouds [S1, S3].
 
